"the daemon. It has no file, no socket and no clock but the one we give it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2351
msgid ""
"``fuel_limit`` (100,000 operations for one call, 1,000 to 100,000,000), "
"``memory_pages`` (4 pages of 64 KiB, 1 to 256), ``host_calls`` (16, at "
"most 1,024). The module is at most 4 MiB."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2352
msgid "``agent``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2353
msgid ""
"A process of ours that listens on the Unix socket ``socket``. Each thread "
"of the daemon keeps one connection to it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2354
msgid ""
"``deadline`` (``5ms``, 1 ms to 1 s), ``queue`` (64 calls of the node that "
"may wait at one time, 1 to 4,096), ``breaker`` (``{\"failures\": 5, "
"\"open\": \"1s\"}``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2356
msgid ""
"*What a failure means.* A call fails when a module spends its fuel or "
"traps, when an agent is away or late, or when a request head does not fit "
"the input buffer of a module or one frame of an agent. Every plugin has "
"``on_failure``, and it is ``\"deny\"`` unless we write otherwise. A "
"failing hook then denies: this is what we want of an authorization hook, "
"and it means that a broken agent refuses the traffic of its frontend. "
"With ``\"on_failure\": \"admit\"`` the hook is advisory. We choose it "
"with care: a client who can make an advisory hook fail, for example with "
"a very long head, passes it. A telemetry hook never refuses anything. Its "
"failure loses one sample."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2358
msgid ""
"*The breaker.* After ``failures`` failed calls in a row a thread stops "
"asking its agent for the time ``open``. Calls in that time fail at once, "
"without a system call. Then one call goes out as a probe. Its success "
"closes the breaker, and its failure opens it for another interval. Each "
"thread has its own breaker, so a dead agent costs each thread "
"``failures`` deadlines and then one deadline per interval. An agent call "
"at the request stage holds its thread for the round trip. We therefore "
"keep ``deadline`` short and the agent on the same host."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2360
msgid ""
"*Parked connections.* At the connection stage nothing waits for an agent. "
"A connection whose hooks name an agent is parked, and a small set of "
"threads, the judges, asks the agent. Connections of other frontends are "
"not delayed, however slow the agent is. The root object "
"``\"hook_judges\": {\"threads\": 4, \"queue\": 256}`` sets how many "
"judges there are (1 to 64) and how many connections may wait for one (1 "
"to 4,096). When the queue is full a connection is not parked: its agents "
"fail with ``overload``, and ``on_failure`` decides. A slow agent serves "
"about ``threads`` divided by its latency connections in a second, so we "
"raise ``threads`` for an agent that answers in milliseconds. A change of "
"``hook_judges`` takes effect at a restart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2362
msgid ""
"*What a hook cannot do.* It cannot read or change a body, change the "
"path, the method or an existing field, choose a server, or change the "
"response. It cannot add a field of framing, or a field that Sibuna writes "
"as its own statement, such as ``X-Forwarded-For`` or ``X-Sibuna-Status``: "
"such a field is dropped and counted. One call adds at most 8 fields and "
"1,024 bytes. A hook sees request heads with their cookies and "
"authorization fields, so a module and an agent are parties to that data. "
"The mode of the socket file decides who may be the agent."
msgstr ""

